there are more job openings in the u.s  than ever recorded before according to  the labor department the economy had  more than 10 million openings in the  month of june up from 9.5 million in may  and last month linkedin saw a 113  percent spike in job openings in this  country compared with a year ago now  linkedin career expert catherine fisher  joins us now with some tips on getting  the job you want out of that 10 million  that are available uh and catherine i  was really struck by this survey that  linkedin did finding that what people  are looking for is not necessarily pay  in fact that's fourth on their list so  what do they want and how do they get it  so what they want really is flexibility  and what's happened is that over this  last year and a half people have  redefined what's most important to them  and flexibility is becoming the one of  the most exciting things more than pay  yes more than pay health benefits  culture  so this really shows you that this is  why companies are really trying to react  to this so when you're looking for that  job it's going to be really important to  make sure that if flexibility is the  most most important thing to you how are  those companies really addressing that  need of yours i felt that when i was  younger i cared about pay but now when  you get older i do care more about  flexibility in terms of i do think that  that's true yeah maybe they'll move the  hours of this show to 11-1  that would be so great that would be so  great so how do you put a resume  together to get the job you want once  you realize this is it yeah you really  want to lead with those skills so what's  happening is you know companies are  wanting to make sure that you can do the  job but you also want to show those soft  skills so how do you do the job they  they're assuming that if you're applying  for it you you kind of check most of the  boxes so make sure you're looking at  that job description and that you  include those skills another one that  may not be so obvious but is really  important is check for typos you would  not believe how many people have typos  that is your best foot forward so make  sure you're doing everything to look  great what should you leave off the  resume i would say probably uh your  salary requirements and here's another  thing not to do is don't leave just like  a full job description for every  position you've had right what you want  to do is really show like the  accomplishments that you had in those  roles so did you you know instead of  saying i manage the front office you can  say i put in systems that you know  improved productivity by ten percent  that really shows that what the what  you've accomplished in those roles one  of the tricky moments of every job  interview is when the interviewer says  do you have any questions for me yes you  better come up with a better comment  yeah i i never think it's good to say  nope don't have any questions  think of something i've often heard too  that people make a decision about you  the first three seconds you walk in the  door  your picture clips on yeah first  impressions always make a  you don't get a second chance to make a  first impression yes so what's the main  thing you think that grabs people right  away and says i know i like him i like  her it's having really intuitive  questions for the interviewer so making  sure that as you're going in there  they're interviewing you but you're also  interviewing them so they want to make  sure that you're really interested in  this role and want to learn more about  it and i would always ask questions like  how do you measure success you know what  is what are the team dynamics do you  work collaboratively or is it more  siloed work so really probing about what  it's like to work there and then  illustrating the skills that you have so  if you've made it to the interview they  know that you can do the job but really  show examples of how you do that work  so many of these job interviews now are  zoom interviews they're virtual any tips  for handling those because they feel  different you can't make a connection as  easily yeah so one of this is going to  sound awkward but try to make that  connection look at the camera  and you don't want to be like in the  side you know primping your hair that's  a real dead giveaway um so make sure  that you're looking at the camera so  that you feel that connection make sure  that your background is nice and clean  so that it's not distracting because  what you want is you want them to be  focused on your story not what's going  on behind you oh really good advice  catherine fisher thank you so much for  being with us on a hot job market
 

